&lt;p&gt;The Lakers are playing the Celtics in the NBA finals. To win the NBA finals, a team must win 4 games before the other team does. If the Celtics win each game with probability $\dfrac{2}{3}$ and there are no ties, what is the probability that the Lakers will win the NBA finals but that the contest will need all seven games to be decided? Express your answer as a fraction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let's denote the probability of the Lakers winning a game as $P(L)$ and the probability of the Celtics winning a game as $P(C)$. We are interested in the probability that the Lakers win the NBA finals but that the contest will need all seven games to be decided. This means that the Lakers win the first game, the Celtics win the second game, the Lakers win the third game, the Celtics win the fourth game, and the Lakers win the fifth game. The probability of this happening is given by the product of the individual probabilities: $P(L) \times P(C) \times P(L) \times P(C) \times P(L) = \left(\dfrac{2}{3}\right)^5 = \dfrac{32}{243}$ (rounded to the nearest integer). Therefore, the probability that the Lakers will win the NBA finals but that the contest will need all seven games to be decided is $\dfrac{32}{243}$.&lt;/p&gt;
